<?phem

namespace App\Ui\Demos;

use Phem\Attribute\Page;
use Phem\Node;

use function App\Ui\{DemoPage, Example, Examples, Hero};
use function Phem\Ui\Base\Examples\{AlertDialogControlledDemo, AlertDialogDetachedDemo, AlertDialogFromMenuDemo, AlertDialogHeroDemo, AlertDialogMultipleDemo, DialogCloseConfirmationDemo};

#[Page('/components/alert-dialog')]
function AlertDialogPage(): Node
{
    return (
        <DemoPage slug="alert-dialog">
            <Hero source="AlertDialogHeroDemo">
                <AlertDialogHeroDemo />
            </Hero>
            <Examples>
                <Example title="Open from a menu" source="AlertDialogFromMenuDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>In order to open an alert dialog using a menu, make the menu item a detached trigger of the alert dialog: give it <code>data-phem-dialog-for</code> with the <code>handle</code> of the alert dialog. The click closes the menu and opens the alert dialog.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <AlertDialogFromMenuDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Close confirmation" source="DialogCloseConfirmationDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>This example shows a nested confirmation dialog that opens if the text entered in the parent dialog is going to be discarded.</p>
                        <p>To implement this, give the <code>confirmClose</code> prop of the parent <code>Dialog</code> the <code>handle</code> of an <code>AlertDialog</code> in its popup. When the user changed a field, a request to close opens the alert dialog: the confirmation is automatically shown when the user clicks the backdrop, presses the Esc key, or clicks a close button. Its <code>DialogDiscard</code> button closes the two dialogs.</p>
                        <p>Use the <code>[data-nested-dialog-open]</code> selector and the <code>var(--nested-dialogs)</code> CSS variable to customize the styling of the parent dialog. Backdrops of the child dialogs won't be rendered so that you can present the parent dialog in a clean way behind the one on top of it.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <DialogCloseConfirmationDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Detached triggers" source="AlertDialogDetachedDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>An alert dialog can be controlled by a trigger located either inside or outside the <code>AlertDialog</code> component. For simple, one-off interactions, place the <code>AlertDialogTrigger</code> inside <code>AlertDialog</code>, as shown in the example at the top of this page.</p>
                        <p>However, if defining the alert dialog's content next to its trigger is not practical, you can use a detached trigger. This involves placing the <code>AlertDialogTrigger</code> outside of <code>AlertDialog</code> and linking them with the same <code>handle</code>. The root gets the handle as its <code>id</code>, and the trigger gets <code>data-phem-dialog-for</code>.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <AlertDialogDetachedDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Multiple triggers" source="AlertDialogMultipleDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>A single alert dialog can be opened by multiple trigger elements. You can achieve this by using the same <code>handle</code> for several detached triggers, or by placing multiple <code>AlertDialogTrigger</code> components inside a single <code>AlertDialog</code>.</p>
                        <p>The alert dialog can render different content depending on which trigger opened it. Give each trigger a <code>payload</code>: the <code>AlertDialogPayload</code> part shows the payload of the trigger that opened the alert dialog.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <AlertDialogMultipleDemo />
                </Example>
                <Example title="Controlled mode with multiple triggers" source="AlertDialogControlledDemo">
                    <Example.description>
                        <p>You can control the alert dialog's open state with the <code>defaultOpen</code> and <code>triggerId</code> props of <code>AlertDialog</code>: a render with new values opens the alert dialog for the trigger with that <code>id</code>. The controller keeps the values of the browser in <code>data-phem-dialog-open-value</code> and <code>data-phem-dialog-trigger-id-value</code>.</p>
                        <p>Here, the button is in the root, and opens the alert dialog without a request with the <code>phem-dialog#open</code> action and a <code>triggerId</code> param. With no payload, <code>AlertDialogPayload</code> shows its children.</p>
                    </Example.description>
                    <AlertDialogControlledDemo />
                </Example>
            </Examples>
        </DemoPage>
    );
}
